artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider the nonequilibrium Green's-function (NEGF) formalism on the Keldysh contour for an interacting fermionic many-body system. Let G(1,2) denote the contour-ordered one-particle Green's function and let Σ[G](1,2) be a self-energy obtained from a Φ-derivable (conserving, in the sense of Baym) many-body approximation, such as second-order Born, GW, or T-matrix. The Kadanoff-Baym equations (KBE) are the integro-differential equations

  (i∂_{t_1} − h(1)) G(1,2) − ∫ Σ[G](1,3) G(3,2) d3 = δ(1,2),

together with their adjoint, where h is the one-body Hamiltonian. Such Φ-derivable approximations preserve the macroscopic conservation laws (particle number, total energy, momentum), but are known to exhibit the following pathologies in time evolution:

  (a) breakdown for sufficiently strong two-body interaction;
  (b) an artificial, correlation-induced damping that, in finite systems, drives the evolution to a non-decaying steady state;
  (c) in extended systems, the appearance of arbitrarily long-lived transient states that ultimately decay to a unique steady state of unclear physical status;
  (d) violation of positivity of two-particle correlation functions (i.e., the two-particle reduced density matrix derived from G can fail to be positive semidefinite).

Problem. Identify and characterize, in a mathematically precise way, the physical regimes (interaction strength, density, dimensionality, finite vs. extended geometry, time scales) in which each of the failures (a)–(d) actually occurs for the KBE with second Born, GW, and T-matrix self-energies, and formulate corrective principles—i.e., systematic modifications of Σ[G] or of the KBE evolution—that simultaneously (i) preserve the macroscopic conservation laws, (ii) eliminate the artificial correlation-induced damping in finite systems, (iii) yield physically justified steady states in extended systems, and (iv) guarantee positivity of all two-particle correlation functions along the evolution.
